WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.219
The question your benchmark has to answer.

2
00:00:02.499 --> 00:00:06.104
By the end of week 3 you have a LoRA adapter that seems to work.

3
00:00:06.384 --> 00:00:16.603
Your team is about to ask a concrete question: should the action-item extractor run on this small model, on your own hardware, instead of calling a frontier model’s API?

4
00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:25.629
The answer depends on four numbers and how sure you are of each: quality, cost per task, latency, and the kinds of mistakes each system makes.

5
00:00:25.909 --> 00:00:32.459
A benchmark that answers this is a controlled experiment, and the controls are where most comparisons go wrong.

6
00:00:32.739 --> 00:00:34.339
You need at least three systems.

7
00:00:34.619 --> 00:00:37.861
The frontier model is the incumbent or the alternative.

8
00:00:38.141 --> 00:00:40.424
The fine-tuned model is the candidate.

9
00:00:40.704 --> 00:00:51.136
The untuned base model, served the same way, is the control: it tells you how much of the candidate’s quality came from fine-tuning and how much was already in the base model.

10
00:00:51.416 --> 00:00:54.936
Without it, a reader can’t tell whether your training run did anything.

11
00:00:55.216 --> 00:01:03.835
You built most of the machinery in Studio 3: a versioned test set, code graders, paired statistics, a decision written before you run.

12
00:01:04.115 --> 00:01:07.805
This lesson adapts it to the situation fine-tuning creates.

13
00:01:08.085 --> 00:01:12.928
The candidate was trained on data you made, so contamination is a live risk.

14
00:01:13.208 --> 00:01:17.880
The systems are served differently, so cost and latency need their own accounting.

15
00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:24.005
And extraction outputs have structure, so a single pass or fail hides most of what you need to know.
